---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';

const facets = [
  {
    title: 'A need, not a luxury',
    text: 'Belonging is a universal, evolutionarily rooted drive to form and keep meaningful connections. In Maslow’s hierarchy, it comes right after food, water and safety. It is felt as being accepted, valued and understood.',
    cite: 'Baumeister & Leary, 1995; Allen, 2020',
  },
  {
    title: 'A process, not a state',
    text: 'Belonging is never fixed. It flickers, depends on context, and is renegotiated all the time. It is shaped by autonomy, choice and commitment, so it can’t be imposed.',
    cite: 'Allen, 2020',
  },
  {
    title: 'Built in small acts',
    text: 'Sharing a thought or a memory, listening, affirming, moving in time with someone. And reframing shared struggles as normal, so that difficulty doesn’t read as proof that you don’t fit in.',
    cite: 'Walton & Cohen, 2011',
  },
  {
    title: 'Quality over quantity',
    text: 'What matters is frequent, warm, stable contact within real mutual care. Going from no close relationships to two changes everything; going from twenty to twenty-two changes very little.',
    cite: 'Baumeister & Leary, 1995',
  },
  {
    title: 'Embodied and placed',
    text: 'Belonging lives in the body and attaches to places, objects, sounds, smells and textures. Think of a place where you belong and every sense comes back at once.',
    cite: 'Allen, 2020',
  },
];

const practices = [
  {
    title: 'Practising together',
    text: 'Compared with meditating alone, studies of group practice report a deeper experience that is easier to sustain. It is also linked with more care for others, empathy and co-regulation of mood.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Sharing afterwards',
    text: 'A sharing round after a practice normalises experience. People discover that what they felt was not theirs alone, and they are listened to without judgement.',
    cite: 'Fagioli et al., 2023; Wyatt et al., 2014',
  },
  {
    title: 'Listening in pairs',
    text: 'Contemplative dyads — one speaks, one listens, then swap — increased perceived social connectedness over months of training.',
    cite: 'Kok & Singer, 2017',
  },
];

const questions = [
  'How do you feel that you belong without feeling that you are being controlled?',
  'What is the difference between having control and playing a role?',
  'Can a room of strangers become a “we” in half an hour — and should it?',
  'Can we belong to weather? To a sky, a cloud, a season?',
  'How do you design for connection that is felt, not performed?',
  'What trace can a one-off encounter leave, so that people might meet again?',
  'When everyone breathes together, where does my breath end?',
];
---

<Base title="Belonging" description="Belonging as a need, a process and an embodied experience — and why we think art and research-creation are good places to explore it.">
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="The theme"
    ink="small"
    title="Belonging"
    lede="We started by asking whether art and technology could help people find a moment of peace. We kept arriving at the same answer: peace is rarely something we find alone."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid-2">
      <blockquote class="big-quote reveal">
        <p>“Our need to belong is like our need for water.”</p>
        <cite>Kelly-Ann Allen, <em>The Psychology of Belonging</em> (2020)</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="lede">
          Belonging is the felt sense of being accepted, included, understood, welcomed, liked and appreciated.
        </p>
        <p>
          Psychology describes it as “a pervasive drive to form and maintain at least a minimum quantity of lasting,
          positive, and significant interpersonal relationships” (Baumeister &amp; Leary, 1995). But reading the
          research as artists, what struck us was how much belonging depends on things a space can hold: small
          rituals, shared attention, the texture of a room, the warmth of light, the chance to be heard.
        </p>
        <p>That makes it a subject for art as much as for science.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Five facets</p>
        <h2>What we mean when we say belonging</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="facets">
        {
          facets.map((f, i) => (
            <li class="facet reveal">
              <span class="num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              <h3>{f.title}</h3>
              <p>{f.text}</p>
              <p class="cite">{f.cite}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark interbeing">
    <div class="wrap grid-2">
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Beyond people</p>
        <h2>Interbeing: there is a cloud in your tea</h2>
        <p>
          Thich Nhat Hanh often pointed to a cup of tea and saw a cloud in it. The cloud has not died: it became
          rain, then a river, then this tea. Nothing is only itself. It <em>inter-is</em> with everything that made it
          possible.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where belonging is warm, social and fragile, interbeing is quiet and continuous. It never really goes
          away. Our project lives between the two. It invites people to feel connected to each other, and also to
          something larger that was here before them and will continue after: weather, water, sky.
        </p>
        <p>
          Belonging in ourselves, with others, and with our environment are always connected. In this work, we lean
          towards the environment, and let the others follow.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="circles reveal" aria-label="Three overlapping circles: ourselves, others, environment">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 320 300" role="img" aria-hidden="true">
          <circle cx="120" cy="115" r="82" class="c1"></circle>
          <circle cx="200" cy="115" r="82" class="c2"></circle>
          <circle cx="160" cy="185" r="96" class="c3"></circle>
          <text x="88" y="98" text-anchor="middle">ourselves</text>
          <text x="232" y="98" text-anchor="middle">others</text>
          <text x="160" y="240" text-anchor="middle">environment</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Where mindfulness meets belonging</p>
        <h2>Practices that make room for each other</h2>
        <p class="lede measure">
          Our project began in contemplative practice. The literature suggests that some of its most powerful
          effects are social.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3">
        {
          practices.map((p) => (
            <article class="practice reveal">
              <h3>{p.title}</h3>
              <p>{p.text}</p>
              {p.cite && <p class="cite">{p.cite}</p>}
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section why">
    <div class="wrap grid-2">
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why art, why research-creation</p>
        <h2>Belonging can’t be streamed</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <p>
          You can read about belonging, but you can’t download it. It happens between bodies in a shared place and a
          shared time. That is exactly what installations, rituals and performances are good at: they gather people,
          suspend ordinary rules for a while, and give attention something to rest on together.
        </p>
        <p>
          Research-creation lets us do both at once. We make something, invite people into it, and listen carefully
          to what they experience: through questionnaires, physiology and, above all, their own words. The artwork
          becomes a way of asking a question, and the question keeps reshaping the artwork.
        </p>
        <p>
          We think belonging deserves far more attention from artists and researchers, especially now, when so many
          people feel alone in crowded places.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section questions dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">An open invitation</p>
        <h2>Questions we are living with</h2>
        <p class="lede measure">
          We don’t have answers to these. If any of them pull at you, take them into your own practice, and tell us
          what you find.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="q-list">
        {questions.map((q) => <li class="reveal">{q}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <div class="actions reveal">
        <a class="button" href="/installation/">See how we approached them</a>
        <a class="button" href="/toolkit/">Use the belonging checklist</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .big-quote {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
  }
  .big-quote p {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.4rem + 2.6vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    font-weight: 300;
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }
  .big-quote cite {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .section-head {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .facets {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(6, 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--line);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .facet {
    background: var(--paper);
    padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
    grid-column: span 2;
  }
  .facet:nth-child(n + 4) {
    grid-column: span 3;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .facet,
    .facet:nth-child(n + 4) {
      grid-column: span 3;
    }
    .facet:last-child {
      grid-column: span 6;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .facet,
    .facet:nth-child(n + 4) {
      grid-column: span 6;
    }
  }
  .num {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--dusk);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  .facet p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 0.97rem;
  }
  .cite {
    font-size: 0.8rem !important;
    color: var(--ink-faint) !important;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .interbeing {
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse at 80% 30%, #344589, var(--night) 70%);
  }
  .interbeing p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .circles {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
  }
  .circles svg {
    width: min(100%, 26rem);
  }
  .circles circle {
    fill-opacity: 0.18;
    stroke-width: 1;
    stroke-opacity: 0.6;
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }
  .c1 {
    fill: #f8a98c;
    stroke: #f8a98c;
  }
  .c2 {
    fill: #9a9dea;
    stroke: #9a9dea;
  }
  .c3 {
    fill: #f9c77c;
    stroke: #f9c77c;
    fill-opacity: 0.28 !important;
  }
  .circles text {
    fill: #f1f2fc;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 15px;
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .practice {
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .practice p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .questions {
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--night) 0%, #3a3478 100%);
  }
  .q-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0 0 3rem;
    padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .q-list li {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 1rem + 1.2vw, 2rem);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.3;
    padding-block: 1.2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.8rem;
  }
</style>
